 Let $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix},$ $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix},$ and $\mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} c_1 \\ c_2 \\ c_3 \end{pmatrix}.$  Then the volume of the parallelepiped determined by $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ is
\[4 = |\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c})|.\][1] Now, let $A = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b},$ $B = \mathbf{b} + 3 \mathbf{c},$ and $C = \mathbf{c} - 7 \mathbf{a}.$  Then the volume of the parallelepiped determined by $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ is
\[|\mathbf{A} \cdot (\mathbf{B} \times \mathbf{C})|.\]Using the property $\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) = \mathbf{b} \cdot (\mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a}),$ we can express this as
|\mathbf{A} \cdot (\mathbf{B} \times \mathbf{C})| &= |\mathbf{B} \cdot (\mathbf{C} \times \mathbf{A})| \\
&= |\mathbf{B} \cdot ((\mathbf{b} + 3 \mathbf{c}) \times (\mathbf{c} - 7 \mathbf{a}))| \\
&= |\mathbf{B} \cdot ((\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) - 7 \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a} + 3 \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{c} - 21 \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a})| \\
&= |\mathbf{B} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} + 7 \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} - 21 \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a})| \\
&= |\mathbf{B} \cdot (7 \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} + 7 \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} + 7 \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a})| \\
&= 7 |\mathbf{B} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{c} \times \mathbf{a})|.
\]Since the last expression is the scalar triple product, it follows that $|\mathbf{A} \cdot (\mathbf{B} \times \mathbf{C})| = 7 \cdot 4 = \boxed{28}.$  (To see that the scalar triple product is linear with respect to the entries in the basic vectors, consider the expansion into the terms
\[(a_2 b_3 - b_2 a_3) c_1 + (b_1 c_2 - c_1 b_2) a_3 + (c_1 a_2 - a_1 c_2) b_3.\]This expression shows that the scalar triple product is in fact a cubic form involving the 3 entries in the basic vectors, and thus is linear in each of the basic vectors.  Also, one can expand the scalar triple product this way by expanding separately, then recombining rather than using its cyclic symmetry.)

